equired fields, valid backend combinations per the validation report).\n\nHLR Traceability: HLR-1..HLR-8 (primary implementing epic for all HLRs).\n\n### E005 — docs-authoring: Admin guide documentation\n\nDescription: RHOAI admin guide documentation for the Feature Store admin UI workflow: deploying, monitoring, inspecting, deleting FeatureStore instances from the dashboard, including disconnected deployment instructions.\n\nScope (content outline inside Scope):\n- New admin guide topic: deploying a Feature Store instance via the dashboard create wizard (field-by-field guidance).\n- Topic: monitoring and inspecting instances (status meanings, detail view).\n- Topic: deleting instances and cleanup behavior.\n- Topic: viewer (non-admin) read-only experience.\n- Topic: disconnected/air-gapped deployment instructions.\n\nACs:\n- Admin guide documents the full workflow: create, monitor, inspect, delete.\n- Disconnected deployment instructions included.\n- Technically reviewed against the implemented UI by engineering (Rule 24).\n- Published in the RHOAI admin guide.\n\nHLR Traceability: Supports HLR-1..HLR-7 documentation coverage (docs epic; primary coverage in implementation).\n\nHmm — for docs epic HLR traceability: docs don't implement HLRs. I'll write \"Documents the workflows delivered by HLR-1 through HLR-7; no direct HLR implementation.\"\n\n### BRANCH-B-E006 — Implementation: feast-operator remediation (conditional)\n\nOnly exists if the validation investigation finds gaps. Description: remediate feast-operator/FeatureStore CRD gaps identified by the validation — e.g., webhook validation insufficient for UI-submitted resources, or reconciliation/cleanup gaps for dashboard-created CRs.\n\nACs:\n- Identified gap(s) are fixed in feast-operator (or CRD), with changes following the upstream-first workflow (feast-dev/feast → opendatahub-io midstream → red-hat-data-services downstream).\n- Dashboard-submitted FeatureStore CRs that violate schema constraints are rejected with retrievable error messages.\n- Operator reconciles and cleans up dashboard-created CRs identically to kubectl-created ones.\n\nPriority P0. branch=B, gated_by E001, dependencies E001, gate_failure_impact.action=add_remediation, fallback_approach: \"If validation confirms the operator and CRD already handle dashboard-created resources correctly, this remediation epic is not needed and is dropped; the main-plan dashboard implementation proceeds on the existing feast-operator.\"\n\nNow frontmatter for each. Also ai-signals rationale files:\n- RHAISTRAT-8007-E001-ai-signals.md (investigation signals)\n- E002, E003, E004, E005, BRANCH-B-E006 ai-signals files.\n\nWait — does the validator or contract require ai-signals files? The prompt says write one per epic. The validator doesn't check them, but the contract says a branch epic missing its ai-signals file fails the artifact contract. I'll write all 6.\n\nLet me now write everything. First the decomposition summary body.\n\nAlso: \"epic_count\" — 5 main-plan (+1 branch). critical_path_length=3.\n\nLet me write the files now. I'll use Write tool for bodies, then frontmatter.py set for FM.\n\nOrder: summary body → set summary FM (without decompose_complete) → epic bodies → epic FM → ai-signals files → batch-read → validator → fix → set decompose_complete=true (final action).\n\nNote the pipeline constraint: \"After launching each wave of agents, your next Bash call MUST be wait-for-wave\" — I'm not launching agents, so N/A.\n\nLet me write the summary now.","signature":"ac77cd1cc3654f2a8eb78a8a5d67843f"}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":42556,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-866fdf02f8c7596b","agent_id":"a91c29e841bf56c68","session_id":"f4ee95a6-a773-43fc-83d6-721cce8a6ac9","uuid":"44168f4b-6e52-47aa-a149-2938fc61f110","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:10:35.262Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_descript