{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-ada82aa2-167f-4179-8480-7b0a376892f9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-8221a6d42c36c95f","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-8007-55wpkne0/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8007-E001.md","content":"# Validate FeatureStore CRD and feast-operator support for dashboard-driven lifecycle\n\n## Description\n\nThis investigation resolves the open questions that determine the final scope of the Feature Store admin UI: which `FeatureStore` CRD (feast.dev/v1) fields the dashboard create wizard should expose for Technology Preview, which store backend combinations should be restricted, and whether the feast-operator and its CRD/webhook validation correctly handle FeatureStore custom resources submitted through the dashboard's Kubernetes API pass-through (rather than via kubectl). It produces a written validation report consumed by the dashboard frontend implementation, and determines whether any remediation work in the feast-operator is required.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Analyze the `FeatureStore` CRD (feast.dev/v1) OpenAPI schema and produce a recommended TP form field set for the create wizard — full spec vs. curated subset — covering registry type, online store backend (Redis, PostgreSQL, DynamoDB, SQLite), offline store backend, authorization mode (OIDC vs. Kubernetes RBAC), TLS settings, scaling configuration, and materialization scheduling. Flag fields that are safe to default or omit for TP, with rationale.\n- Recommend store backend combination restrictions for the form (e.g., SQLite as development-only / not production-suitable), identifying which combinations are production-ready.\n- Verify (or identify gaps in) feast-operator reconciliation of FeatureStore CRs created via the Kubernetes API — confirming the operator watches the CRD regardless of creation method (dashboard, kubectl, GitOps) and that finalizer-based cleanup removes all owned resources (Deployments, Services, ConfigMaps, CronJobs, HPAs, PDBs, RBAC, Routes).\n- Verify (or identify gaps in) CRD/webhook validation behavior for UI-submitted resources: whether the API server rejects invalid CRs with error messages the dashboard can retrieve and display to the user.\n- Confirm the `featurestore-editor-role` and `featurestore-viewer-role` ClusterRoles (defined by the feast-operator) grant the exact verb sets required by the admin and viewer authorization tiers, and use the editor role as the reference for the minimum permission set the dashboard ClusterRole extension needs.\n- Assess the engineering prototype (odh-dashboard draft PR #7013) for architectural alignment with this strategy: what can be reused versus rebuilt.\n- Validate the assumption that the feast.dev/v1 CRD is stable for the RHOAI 3.5 cycle (v1alpha1 deprecated; pin to v1).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- A written validation report exists that recommends the TP create-wizard field set, mapping every included and excluded `FeatureStore` CRD field with rationale, and records product-owner sign-off on the curated-subset decision.\n- The report documents store backend combination restrictions with production-readiness rationale for each combination.\n- Reconciliation of dashboard-created FeatureStore CRs is verified on a test cluster, including resource cleanup after deletion — or the gap is documented with a concrete remediation proposal (which triggers the conditional feast-operator remediation plan).\n- API-server/webhook rejection of invalid UI submissions is verified to return retrievable, displayable error messages — or the gap is documented with a remediation proposal.\n- The viewer and editor ClusterRole verb sets are confirmed against the required admin/viewer tiers, with any mismatch documented.\n- The prototype alignment assessment is documented, listing reusable components versus rework.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- Informs HLR-1 (P0 — defines the create wizard's field set).\n- Informs HLR-8 (P2 — defines the CRD schema constraints behind inline validation).\n- De-risks HLR-4 (P0 — validates finalizer cleanup behind the delete flow) and HLR-6 (P1 — confirms the viewer/editor RBAC tiers).\n- This epic does not implement HLRs directly; it gates the dashboard admin UI implementation epics.\n\n## Risks / Notes\n\n- The curated-subset-vs-full-spec decision includes a product judgment call (PM input required); the technical analysis (schema coverage, backend constraints) is separable and proceeds regardless.\n- If reconciliation or webhook-validation gaps are found, the conditional feast-operator remediation plan attached to this investigation is activated.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":50933,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-866fdf02f8c7596b","agent_id":"a91c29e841bf56c68","session_id":"f4ee95a6-a773-43fc-83d6-721cce8a6ac9","uuid":"8e4b237d-6cc8-4911-b667-72faf944ca53","timestamp":"2026-10-22T05:11:23.983Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-8007 into epics"}
